<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vakil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vakil was a Persian title meaning deputy or regent, used for a high office of state in Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The Zand ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> adopted it rather than the title of Shah, presenting himself as regent rather than king. His capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> was adorned with a group of buildings bearing the name, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil-bazaar">Vakil Bazaar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil-mosque">Vakil Mosque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil-bath">Vakil Bath</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultan stood at the head of a court modelled on Persian and Delhi traditions, assisted by ministers such as the <b>vakil</b>-us-sultanat, or chief minister, and the amir-i jumla, in charge of finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike his competitors, Karim Khan refused to take the title of shah, preferring that of <b>Vakil</b>, and later Vakil al-Ruaya ('Deputy of the People'), a deliberate choice that presented his authority as service to the population rather than dynastic self-aggrandisement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rivalry between pen and sword was old: between 1508 and 1524 the Qizilbash brought about the deaths of three of the five Persians whom Ismail I made <b>vakil</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
